WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.338 --> 00:00:07.474
This is a very clear case of,

00:00:07.474 --> 00:00:09.209
if this object didn't get help,

00:00:09.209 --> 00:00:12.912
it would deteriorate even more over time.

00:00:14.147 --> 00:00:17.317
I'm Sara, director of
conservation at Center Art Studio,

00:00:17.317 --> 00:00:19.052
and today I'm going to walk you through

00:00:19.052 --> 00:00:23.156
how we restore a polychrome
sculpture of St. Paul

00:00:23.156 --> 00:00:25.058
from 1720.

00:00:26.059 --> 00:00:27.761
The work that we're doing on St. Paul

00:00:27.761 --> 00:00:29.429
is a full restoration.

00:00:29.429 --> 00:00:31.097
This piece was very dirty.

00:00:31.097 --> 00:00:33.166
It also was flaking,

00:00:33.166 --> 00:00:36.770
so the paint was lifting and cupping.

00:00:36.770 --> 00:00:40.173
It was actively in a state of degradation,

00:00:40.173 --> 00:00:43.176
where any kind of transport
or touching or handling

00:00:43.176 --> 00:00:45.678
of this piece would potentially risk

00:00:45.678 --> 00:00:47.580
incurring additional damage.

00:00:48.715 --> 00:00:53.653
This piece belongs to a private
collector in New York City.

00:00:53.653 --> 00:00:57.090
We know that it's Latin colonial.

00:00:57.090 --> 00:01:01.227
It is carved from a single piece of wood,

00:01:01.227 --> 00:01:04.798
and it's been painted and gilded.

00:01:04.798 --> 00:01:09.436
The book and the sword are
always symbolic of St. Paul,

00:01:09.436 --> 00:01:12.272
so when you see those
attributes on a sculpture

00:01:12.272 --> 00:01:13.907
or in a painting or work of art,

00:01:13.907 --> 00:01:17.644
you can know that that saint
is meant to be St. Paul.

00:01:17.644 --> 00:01:21.481
The writing on the book, it almost looks

00:01:21.481 --> 00:01:25.685
like an early depiction
of music known as neumes.

00:01:25.685 --> 00:01:27.821
That wouldn't be a typical attribute

00:01:27.821 --> 00:01:30.623
of the book that St. Paul holds.

00:01:30.623 --> 00:01:35.261
It stands out in this piece
as being a little bit unusual.

00:01:35.261 --> 00:01:37.630
You never know exactly what conditions

00:01:37.630 --> 00:01:38.965
something has been exposed to,

00:01:38.965 --> 00:01:42.068
so there are certain climatic conditions

00:01:42.068 --> 00:01:43.536
that can cause flaking.

00:01:44.404 --> 00:01:47.340
We also know that because
the paint is flaking

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:49.008
in particular areas,

00:01:49.008 --> 00:01:51.878
that means that the entire
sculpture is at risk.

00:01:52.846 --> 00:01:55.115
The first step of this restoration

00:01:55.115 --> 00:01:58.751
is to consolidate the flaking piece.

00:01:58.751 --> 00:02:01.454
We can't clean the object
until it's stabilized.

00:02:02.388 --> 00:02:05.291
Obviously, it's an
object that has history,

00:02:05.291 --> 00:02:08.027
but it is nicely carved.

00:02:08.895 --> 00:02:11.231
It had beautiful crackle lines,

00:02:11.231 --> 00:02:12.999
and the colors were vibrant.

00:02:12.999 --> 00:02:15.502
So if we remediate those issues,

00:02:15.502 --> 00:02:19.772
we're going to end up with
a very beautiful object.

00:02:19.772 --> 00:02:21.875
The book was heavily flaking.

00:02:21.875 --> 00:02:26.412
It also is just a big
open horizontal plane

00:02:26.412 --> 00:02:29.516
that's going to accumulate a lot of dirt.

00:02:29.516 --> 00:02:31.951
We're going to do the
same consolidation process

00:02:31.951 --> 00:02:33.319
to the feet.

00:02:33.319 --> 00:02:37.524
So, the first thing we have
to do is apply this resin.

00:02:37.524 --> 00:02:41.027
It's a completely stable
conservation-grade resin.

00:02:41.027 --> 00:02:43.463
It's dissolved in a solvent.

00:02:43.463 --> 00:02:45.298
We apply it with a brush,

00:02:45.298 --> 00:02:48.835
and we apply it on the surface,

00:02:48.835 --> 00:02:52.472
and also we try to get under the flakes

00:02:52.472 --> 00:02:56.743
and into the cracks of the
sculpture as best we can.

00:02:57.610 --> 00:02:59.512
Capillary action helps us here.

00:02:59.512 --> 00:03:02.048
So this liquid flows under the chips

00:03:02.048 --> 00:03:04.751
and into the recesses of the sculpture,

00:03:04.751 --> 00:03:07.287
and the sculpture soaks it up.

00:03:07.287 --> 00:03:10.123
That allows the flakes to become flexible,

00:03:10.123 --> 00:03:13.593
and we can kind of push them
down back into position.

00:03:13.593 --> 00:03:15.628
And then over the course of a few hours,

00:03:15.628 --> 00:03:19.199
the solvent that that resin
was dissolved in evaporates,

00:03:19.199 --> 00:03:20.767
but the resin remains.

00:03:20.767 --> 00:03:22.969
And so that stabilizes the piece.

00:03:24.204 --> 00:03:28.107
We use a solvent to
simultaneously remove that resin

00:03:28.107 --> 00:03:31.511
and also clean the dirt off.

00:03:31.511 --> 00:03:34.113
Just that one solvent
removes all those layers

00:03:34.113 --> 00:03:37.584
and just gets us down to
the original paint layer.

00:03:37.584 --> 00:03:40.320
There were felt pads on the bottom

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:42.622
of the base of the sculpture.

00:03:42.622 --> 00:03:45.458
They also can be places
where dirt gets trapped.

00:03:45.458 --> 00:03:47.694
So these were not really even necessary,

00:03:47.694 --> 00:03:48.795
so we just removed them,

00:03:48.795 --> 00:03:53.266
and we'll clean under those
areas and just start fresh.

00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:56.269
Now that the piece has been cleaned,

00:03:56.269 --> 00:03:59.239
we are going to move on
to filling the cracks

00:03:59.239 --> 00:04:01.107
and areas of loss.

00:04:01.107 --> 00:04:04.644
We use a water-based putty to fill in

00:04:04.644 --> 00:04:07.780
any missing areas on the figure,

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:10.550
on the robe, on the base.

00:04:10.550 --> 00:04:14.654
It goes on in sort of a
liquid, viscous state.

00:04:14.654 --> 00:04:18.224
It basically is replacing
the missing gesso.

00:04:18.224 --> 00:04:20.693
We overfill a little bit

00:04:20.693 --> 00:04:24.864
because it is so easily
reversible and reducible

00:04:24.864 --> 00:04:27.667
that you're going to get a more even fill

00:04:27.667 --> 00:04:32.038
if you slightly overfill the area of loss.

00:04:32.872 --> 00:04:35.642
You know, we put it on and
it looks a little wrong,

00:04:35.642 --> 00:04:40.213
but then we can use water
to reduce these fills.

00:04:40.213 --> 00:04:44.017
We also can use a very
fine-grit sandpaper.

00:04:44.017 --> 00:04:46.886
You want to be very careful
that you're using a grit

00:04:46.886 --> 00:04:49.122
that's not going to cause any damage

00:04:49.122 --> 00:04:52.191
to the original painted surface.

00:04:52.191 --> 00:04:54.127
Now that the sculpture has been filled,

00:04:54.127 --> 00:04:56.596
we'll use brushes and conservation paints

00:04:56.596 --> 00:04:59.165
to inpaint those fills and match,

00:04:59.165 --> 00:05:01.534
as best we can, the original paint

00:05:01.534 --> 00:05:03.536
that would have been in those places.

00:05:03.536 --> 00:05:06.739
We use conservation paints
when we're doing inpainting.

00:05:06.739 --> 00:05:11.311
And inpainting is simply
applying paint to fills.

00:05:11.311 --> 00:05:13.413
This is different than overpainting,

00:05:13.413 --> 00:05:17.583
which would be painting
over original surfaces.

00:05:17.583 --> 00:05:20.386
It's really a process of color matching

00:05:20.386 --> 00:05:22.922
to the surrounding area.

00:05:22.922 --> 00:05:24.657
My approach when I'm doing it

00:05:24.657 --> 00:05:27.427
is to typically do washes of color.

00:05:27.427 --> 00:05:30.530
So I'll put a color on a fill,

00:05:30.530 --> 00:05:34.133
and then I'll be like, OK,
this needs to simultaneously be

00:05:34.133 --> 00:05:37.437
more red, green, yellow, and brown.

00:05:37.437 --> 00:05:39.806
So then I'll add those colors to my brush,

00:05:39.806 --> 00:05:41.140
and then I'll do it again.

00:05:41.140 --> 00:05:44.310
So it's a series of washes that accumulate

00:05:44.310 --> 00:05:47.714
to create the desired color.

00:05:47.714 --> 00:05:51.184
It goes from looking like
a slightly broken object

00:05:51.184 --> 00:05:54.287
in a state of repair to
looking like a complete object

00:05:54.287 --> 00:05:56.823
quite quickly once all
the inpainting happens.

00:05:56.823 --> 00:06:00.560
So that transition is
very satisfying to see.

00:06:01.961 --> 00:06:03.796
All the areas were stabilized

00:06:03.796 --> 00:06:08.134
and filled and color-matched correctly.

00:06:08.134 --> 00:06:10.970
I think we did a very good job

00:06:10.970 --> 00:06:14.240
in making it be a more complete object.

00:06:16.976 --> 00:06:19.011
Now that we are done with the inpainting,

00:06:19.011 --> 00:06:21.748
we apply shellac to the sculpture.

00:06:21.748 --> 00:06:23.449
We apply it with a brush

00:06:23.449 --> 00:06:28.221
all over the whole sculpture
and allow it to dry.

00:06:28.221 --> 00:06:30.790
After the final coat is
applied to the sculpture,

00:06:30.790 --> 00:06:32.759
we'll wipe it with a cloth, buff it,

00:06:32.759 --> 00:06:36.596
and make sure that
there's no surface dust.

00:06:36.596 --> 00:06:40.266
This restoration took a few
weeks for us to complete.

00:06:40.266 --> 00:06:42.735
This piece needed to be conserved

00:06:42.735 --> 00:06:46.439
because of the state of
degradation that it was in.

00:06:47.573 --> 00:06:49.642
I'm very satisfied with this restoration.

00:06:49.642 --> 00:06:51.911
A lot of time went into it.

00:06:52.945 --> 00:06:56.682
I'm very happy with how the
whole project turned out.

00:06:56.682 --> 00:06:59.051
And really, it's always very satisfying

00:06:59.051 --> 00:07:02.822
to be able to not only stop damage

00:07:02.822 --> 00:07:07.026
but prevent future damage
from happening on an object.

00:07:07.026 --> 00:07:08.428
It just makes us feel

00:07:08.428 --> 00:07:11.931
like we are helping extend the longevity

00:07:11.931 --> 00:07:14.567
of a work of art through
the work that we do.

